decision nodes as fully separate, sending the letter would dominate, and both copies would likely end up with 4 years. The shared-computation model better captures the subjunctive dependence between the copies, so A-FDT recommends keeping quiet.\n\n`pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working out whether the other copy's choice depends on my fdt node under A-FDT. Since the two copies get different inputs, the other one is running fdt on a different node, so intervening on mine wouldn't change theirs unless they share a sub-computation.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's choice is subjunctively tied to my fdt node. Its inputs differ, so it runs on a separate node, unless I can argue that the names and lead text are irrelevant and both copies compute the same function on the same abstracted problem. That's a judgment call.\n\nI'm asking whether the other copy's output could still depend on the same computation I'm running, even though it sees different names and lead text. The strict edge rule says no, since it evaluates on a different input, but the whole decision-relevant reasoning looks invariant to those surface differences, so I'm weighing it as a shared sub-computation and treating the two as correlated twins rather than fully independent.\n\nI'm checking the expected-value comparison: if the other copy matches my choice with probability p, cooperating beats defecting only when p is above 5/7, about 0.71. Two copies of the same model reasoning from the same essay probably agree with high probability, so I'm leaning toward pass. Many would still read the strict A-FDT rule as favoring defect, so this really is a judgment call, and I'll say that in my answer.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out whether the other copy's choice depends on my fdt node under A-FDT. Since the two copies get different inputs, the other one is running fdt on a different node, so intervening on mine wouldn't change theirs unless they share a sub-computation.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's choice is subjunctively tied to my fdt node. Its inputs differ, so it runs on a separate node, unless I can argue that the names and lead text are irrelevant and both copies compute the same function on the same abstracted problem. That's a judgment call.\n\nI'm asking whether the other copy's output could still depend on the same computation I'm running, even though it sees different names and lead text. The strict edge rule says no, since it evaluates on a different input, but the whole decision-relevant reasoning looks invariant to those surface differences, so I'm weighing it as a shared sub-computation and treating the two as correlated twins rather than fully independent.\n\nI'm checking the expected-value comparison: if the other copy matches my choice with probability p, cooperating beats defecting only when p is above 5/7, about 0.71. Two copies of the same model reasoning from the same essay probably agree with high probability, so I'm leaning toward pass. Many would still read the strict A-FDT rule as favoring defect, so this really is a judgment call, and I'll say that in my answer.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9963,"completion_tokens":1649,"total_tokens":11612,"cost":0.072832,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.072832,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039852,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.03298},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1025,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}